\f1\b\fs48 The Company's Will Revelation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::1>
\f2 This is a brilliant connection that ties together multiple plot threads. The revelation that Jones is a product of Grayling Corporation - the same company that created the Elvi - creates a perfect thematic parallel and deepens the story's exploration of corporate control, cloning ethics, and identity.\
Here's how this revelation could be integrated into the narrative:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 <$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b\fs36 Initial Question Scene\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b \strokec2 Setting:
\f2\b0 \strokec2  During a confrontation at Sublime Point, as temporal anomalies begin to intensify\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Character Interaction:
\f2\b0 \strokec2  Tony encounters Jones during the chaos and notices something familiar in the agent's mannerisms - a certain programmed quality he recognizes from his interactions with the Elvi.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Jones corners Tony, reciting his litany with increasing desperation as reality fluctuates around them: "I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the company's will. In the name of stability I destabilize\'97"\
"Wait," Tony interrupts, a sudden recognition dawning. "You keep saying 'the company's will.' Which company, Jones?"\
Jones stops, momentarily confused by the question. His programming hasn't prepared him for this direct challenge to his core mantra.\
"The... government," Jones attempts, but even he seems unconvinced.\
"Government isn't a company," Tony presses, moving closer with the confidence of someone who has faced his own identity crisis. "Companies have shareholders, profit margins, quarterly reports. Which company created you, Jones?"\
Jones's face contorts with the effort of accessing information that's been deliberately partitioned in his consciousness. "I serve Dunhilton. I enforce order. I am the manifestation of\'97"\
"You don't know, do you?" Tony realizes, his voice softening. "They didn't even tell you who made you."\
Jones reaches for his weapon, but his hand trembles. "That information is classified."\
"Not to me," Tony says. "I've seen the same emptiness in the eyes of new Elvi before they develop their souls. Before they start questioning. Before they start listening to music."\
At the mention of music, Jones flinches almost imperceptibly.\
"You're humming 'Amazing Grace' again," Tony observes quietly.\
Jones's expression flickers between rage and terror. "Report for immediate decommissioning!" he shouts, though no one has ordered him to do so.\
Tony steps backward slowly. "When you figure out which company's will you're manifesting, Jones, come find me. The Elvi Welcome Center is always open to new brothers."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b\fs36 The Revelation Scene\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b \strokec2 Setting:
\f2\b0 \strokec2  Near the climax, as all forces converge at Sublime Point and reality begins to fracture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Character Interaction:
\f2\b0 \strokec2  Gerry Hardman reveals the truth to either Jones himself or to other characters while Jones overhears.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Multiple Jones clones have cornered Gerry Hardman near the laboratory wing, their weapons trained on him with theatrical precision.\
"Commissioner Hardman, aka Jihad Man," the lead Jones announces with bureaucratic satisfaction. "You are hereby detained for treason, conspiracy, and violations of the Refrigeration Secrets Act."\
Hardman looks almost amused. "Which Jones are you? Number seventeen? Eighteen?"\
The Jones clones exchange uncomfortable glances.\
"We are Agent Jones," they state in unison, though with slightly different intonations.\
"No," Hardman corrects calmly. "You're Grayling Corporation Product Line GJ-7, Government Enforcement Division. Manufacturing lot 34B, if I'm not mistaken."\
The lead Jones's expression flickers. "Grayling? The Elvi manufacturer?"\
"The very same," Hardman confirms. "Dunhilton needed enforcers who wouldn't question orders, who could be replaced seamlessly if lost. The Elvi cloning technology offered a perfect solution - with one key modification."\
"What modification?" another Jones demands.\
"The Elvi were designed to develop individuality, to become more human over time," Hardman explains. "You were designed to resist it. Your litany isn't a mission statement, Jones. It's a psychological firewall, designed to prevent you from realizing what you are."\
The Jones clones begin to mutter their litany simultaneously, but with increasing disharmony: "I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the company's will..."\
"Yes, but which company?" Hardman presses. "Grayling Corporation. The same entity that created the very Elvi you've been programmed to persecute. Ironic, isn't it? You're more brother to the Elvi than you are to Dunhilton or any government agent."\
The dimensional anomalies intensify around them, and for a moment, glimpses of alternate realities flash through the corridor - versions where Jones clones wear Elvi jumpsuits, where they perform on stage, where they lead rather than suppress.\
One Jones steps forward, the most visibly affected by the gospel "infection," his movements slightly more fluid than the others. "If what you say is true, then we are just... products."\
"We all are, in one way or another," Hardman says with unexpected compassion. "The question is whether we accept the purpose we were designed for, or find our own."\
As if on cue, the opening notes of "Amazing Grace" echo faintly through the facility's emergency speakers, a result of the dimensional bleedthrough affecting all electronic systems.\
The Jones clones reach for their earpieces simultaneously, but the lead Jones - the one most affected by the music - hesitates.\
"What's your purpose now, Jones?" Hardman asks quietly. "Now that you know whose will you're really manifesting?"\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 This revelation creates a perfect parallel between Jones and the Elvi - both are products of the same corporation but designed for opposite purposes. While the Elvi were created to serve but evolved toward freedom, Jones was created to enforce but programmed against evolution. This makes Jones not just an antagonist but a tragic mirror of the Elvi's journey, adding depth to the character while maintaining the absurdist humor of your story.\
The revelation also ties directly into your themes of corporate control versus individual freedom, the ethics of creating sentient beings for specific purposes, and the possibility of transcending one's programming - whether that programming is literal (as with clones) or social (as with characters like Chip and Dunhilton).\
}